# Keeping Everything in Memory

The data structures discussed so far in this chapter have all been answers to the limitations of disks.

Disks are awkward to work with compared to main memory. With both magnetic disks and SSDs, data needs to be laid out carefully to achieve good read and write performance.

Disks have two significant advantages: they are **durable**, and they have a lower cost per gigabyte than RAM.

Many databases are not that large, so it's feasible to keep them entirely in memory, potentially distributed across several machines. This has led to the development of **in-memory databases**.

Writing to disk has an operational advantage: files on disk can easily be backed up, inspected, and analyzed by external utilities.

Another interesting use case for in-memory databases is providing data models that are difficult to implement with disk-based indexes. For example, Redis offers a database-like interface to various data structures, such as priority queues and sets. Because it keeps data in memory, its implementation is comparatively simple.
